  together.

  [1mFirst, a scope flag.[0m The repo is one static file:

  index.html   ← <h1>Tasks</h1> and an empty <main>

  No tasks, no task model, no users, no persistence, no JS, no server, no build tooling. "Notify users when tasks they
  care about change" presupposes four things that don't exist yet: tasks, users, a notion of [3mcaring about[0m a task
  (subscription/ownership), and a change event to observe. A notifications system built before those exist would be
  designing against imaginary interfaces.

  So before I ask about notifications, I need to know what's actually real.

  ---

  [1mThe fork:[0m where task state lives decides whether "notification" means a DOM toast or a delivery pipeline — and they
  share almost no code.

  - [1mBrowser-only, single user[0m — tasks in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, "change" is a local mutation you just made. Notifications are an
    in-page toast/badge system, ~a day's work, no backend. Rules out notifying anyone who isn't looking at the tab.
  - [1mBackend exists / is planned elsewhere[0m — I design the notification subsystem against that API's event surface.
    Cheapest correct path [3mif[0m the API is real; I'd need to see it.
  - [1mMulti-user app, server to be built[0m — tasks, users, and auth are prerequisites. Notifications become a real pipeline
    (events → subscriptions → fan-out → delivery). This is several sub-projects, and notifications is the [3mlast[0m one, not
    the first.

  Expensive to change later: the multi-user boundary. Going browser-only and adding real users afterward means rewriting
  storage, identity, and the whole delivery path. Cheap to change later: which channels you deliver on (toast, email,
  push) — that's a pluggable seam in any of the three.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me it's the third one only if you mean it. If this is a real product, we decompose —
  tasks/users first, notifications after — and I brainstorm the first sub-project properly. If it's a demo or prototype,
  browser-only gets you something working today and is honest about its limits.

